Chapter-SSH Management
=========================
1. How passwordess authentication established in linux ?

Step 1: Generate SSH Key (Client Side)
ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096
         Creates:
            ~/.ssh/id_rsa       # private key (SECRET)
            ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub   # public key (SHARED)

Step 2: Copy Public Key to Server
ssh-copy-id user@server
         Public key gets added to:
            ~/.ssh/authorized_keys   # on server and server looks into ~/.ssh/authorized_keys at the time of connection from client

Step 3: Set Proper Permissions (IMPORTANT)
chmod 700 ~/.ssh
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

2. What all parameters we can modify in /etc/ssh/sshd_config file ?

PasswordAuthentication no        # disable password login (force key-based auth)
PubkeyAuthentication yes         # enable SSH key-based login
PermitRootLogin no               # disable direct root login
AllowUsers user1 user2           # allow only specific users
DenyUsers baduser                # block specific users

Port 2222                        # change default SSH port (default 22)
MaxAuthTries 3                   # limit login attempts
LoginGraceTime 30                # timeout for login

AuthorizedKeysFile .ssh/authorized_keys   # location of public keys
StrictModes yes                  # enforce correct permissions on SSH files

UsePAM yes                       # enable PAM authentication (Linux auth system)
ChallengeResponseAuthentication no   # disable challenge-response auth

AllowGroups devops               # allow only specific groups
DenyGroups testgrp              # block specific groups

LogLevel INFO                   # logging level
SyslogFacility AUTHPRIV         # log facility

3. How to disable SSH root login completely ?
Edit SSH config:
PermitRootLogin no         # disables root login via SSH
Apply changes:
systemctl restart sshd

4. How to allow only key-based authentication (no password) ?
Edit SSH config:
PasswordAuthentication no
PubkeyAuthentication yes
Restart SSH:
systemctl restart sshd

5. How SSH works internally during authentication ?
Step 1: Client sends request to server
Step 2: Server checks ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
Step 3: Server sends challenge
Step 4: Client signs with private key
Step 5: Server verifies using public key
Step 6: Access granted

6. How to troubleshoot SSH connection issues ?
Check SSH service:
systemctl status sshd
Check logs:
tail -f /var/log/secure        # RHEL/CentOS
tail -f /var/log/auth.log      # Ubuntu
Test verbose:
ssh -v user@server

7. How to change default SSH port ?
Edit config:
Port 2222
Allow in firewall:
firewall-cmd --add-port=2222/tcp --permanent
firewall-cmd --reload
Restart SSH:
systemctl restart sshd

8. How to restrict SSH access by IP address ?
Edit /etc/hosts.allow:
sshd: 192.168.1.0/24
Edit /etc/hosts.deny:
sshd: ALL
(OR using firewall)
firewall-cmd --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="192.168.1.10" accept'

9. How to enable SSH login for specific group only ?
Edit config:
AllowGroups devops
Add user to group:
usermod -aG devops user1

10. How to generate key without passphrase (for automation) ?
ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096 -N ""
👉 Used in:

Ansible
Jenkins
Automation scripts


11. What is known_hosts file in SSH ?
Location:
~/.ssh/known_hosts
Purpose:
✔ Stores server fingerprints
✔ Prevents MITM attacks
Command to remove old key:
ssh-keygen -R server_ip

12. How to enable SSH banner (security warning) ?
Create banner file:
vi /etc/ssh/banner.txt
Edit SSH config:
Banner /etc/ssh/banner.txt
Restart:
systemctl restart sshd

 SSH Troubleshooting Scenarios (Real Production Cases)

1. SSH Connection Refused
Error:
ssh: connect to host <ip> port 22: Connection refused

Root Cause:

SSH service not running
Port blocked

Fix:
systemctl status sshd
systemctl start sshd

ss -tulnp | grep ssh
firewall-cmd --list-all


2. Permission Denied (Publickey)
Error:
Permission denied (publickey).

Root Cause:

Wrong key / key not copied
Permission issue

Fix:
chmod 700 ~/.ssh
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

ssh-copy-id user@server


3. Too Many Authentication Failures
Error:
Received disconnect: Too many authentication failures

Root Cause:

SSH trying multiple keys

Fix:
ssh -i id_rsa user@server

OR edit:
~/.ssh/config
IdentitiesOnly yes


4. Host Key Verification Failed
Error:
WARNING: REMOTE HOST IDENTIFICATION HAS CHANGED!

Root Cause:

Server reinstalled / key changed

Fix:
ssh-keygen -R <server_ip>


5. Connection Timeout
Error:
ssh: connect to host <ip> port 22: Connection timed out

Root Cause:

Network issue / firewall

Fix:
ping <ip>
telnet <ip> 22
firewall-cmd --list-ports


6. Slow SSH Login
Root Cause:

DNS lookup delay

Fix:
Edit config:
UseDNS no

Restart:
systemctl restart sshd


7. Key-based login not working but password works
Root Cause:

Pubkey disabled

Fix:
PubkeyAuthentication yes
AuthorizedKeysFile .ssh/authorized_keys


8. SSH hangs after login
Root Cause:

.bashrc / profile issue

Fix:
ssh -t user@server bash --noprofile --norc

Check:
~/.bashrc
~/.bash_profile


9. SELinux blocking SSH key access (RHEL)
Root Cause:

wrong context

Fix:
restorecon -Rv ~/.ssh

OR:
ls -Z ~/.ssh


10. User unable to SSH but exists
Root Cause:

shell disabled

Check:
cat /etc/passwd | grep user

Fix:
usermod -s /bin/bash user


